00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:01.310
(Transcribed by TurboScribe.ai. Go Unlimited to remove this message.) Hey, welcome to The Explainer.

00:00:01.670 --> 00:00:03.690
Today, we're gonna dive into a really powerful

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:04.770
tool for looking at the future.

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:07.390
But look, this isn't some crystal ball that

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:09.490
gives you one single perfect answer.

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:12.130
No, this is a method for understanding the

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:14.130
thousands of different ways things could play out.

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:16.010
It's called the Monte Carlo simulation.

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:18.250
And it all starts by challenging an idea

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:19.450
we all kind of take for granted.

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:21.610
So let me start by asking you a

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:21.890
question.

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:24.210
When you're making a big decision, is getting

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:27.090
a single, super precise forecast actually a good

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:27.310
thing?

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:29.450
I mean, we're always told to know the

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:30.210
numbers, right?

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:32.190
But what if the most important thing is

00:00:32.190 --> 00:00:34.230
knowing what those numbers can't tell you?

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:36.470
Look, we all love certainty.

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:39.690
It's just human nature, a single solid number.

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:42.350
It feels reliable, it feels final.

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:44.570
And that's how most business plans work, right?

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:47.130
They're what you'd call deterministic models.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:48.910
You plug in your best guess for your

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:51.750
inputs and you get one specific outcome, clean

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:52.370
and simple.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:55.170
So imagine you're starting a food truck.

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.910
You've built this beautiful spreadsheet, you plug in

00:00:57.910 --> 00:00:59.850
your numbers, and it spits out this perfect,

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:03.069
clean number, a projected annual profit of exactly

00:01:03.069 --> 00:01:04.150
$75,000.

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:05.690
Feels great, doesn't it?

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:07.470
You've got a clear target to aim for.

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:09.110
But here's the catch.

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:11.750
That precision, it's really just an illusion.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:13.110
It's a house of cards.

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:14.230
Because think about it.

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:16.530
Every single one of your inputs, how many

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:18.870
sales you'll make, your food costs, even how

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:20.650
many days it might rain, it was all

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:21.910
just a single best guess.

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:24.110
And in the real world, things are never

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:24.650
that simple.

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:26.870
That single number isn't just fraggle.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:28.950
It's hiding the entire story from you.

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:31.410
And this leads us right into something called

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:32.570
the certainty trap.

00:01:32.790 --> 00:01:35.390
It's this powerful, seductive pull of the single

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:36.270
number forecast.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:38.750
It's what everybody does, but you know what?

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:41.050
It can be incredibly dangerous when you're making

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:42.050
important decisions.

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:45.250
So why do single numbers fail so badly?

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:48.350
Well, for one, they completely erase all the

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:50.790
natural ups and downs, all the variability of

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:51.470
the real world.

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:53.710
They can make two projects that are totally

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:56.090
different in terms of risk look exactly the

00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:58.370
same, just because their average outcome is similar.

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:01.030
And, this is the big one, they hide

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:01.930
the tail outcomes.

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:04.350
That's the small but very real chance of

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:07.330
a massive win, or even more importantly, a

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:08.270
catastrophic failure.

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:10.670
And this quote just hits the nail on

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:11.030
the head.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:14.490
Decision makers then optimise a number rather than

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:15.290
manage exposure.

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:17.950
When we get that one target, our whole

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:18.790
mindset shifts.

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:21.390
We stop thinking about managing our overall risk,

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:23.650
and instead we just get obsessed with hitting

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:26.030
that one specific number, even if it means

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:27.930
we're blind to the dangers hiding in plain

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:28.310
sight.

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:30.490
All right, so how do we escape this

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:30.770
trap?

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:32.730
We need a totally new way of thinking,

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:35.170
and it all starts with understanding the crucial

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:36.970
difference between two words that we tend to

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:39.790
use interchangeably, risk and uncertainty.

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:42.070
Think of it like this, risk.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:43.870
That's like a casino game.

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:46.550
We know every single possible outcome, every number

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:48.150
on the roulette wheel, and we can calculate

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:50.030
the exact probability for each one.

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:50.910
It's quantifiable.

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:54.250
But uncertainty, well, that's the real world.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:56.230
That's launching a brand new technology.

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:57.890
We know there's a range of things that

00:02:57.890 --> 00:02:59.710
could happen, but we don't have any solid

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:01.430
historical data to pin down the probabilities.

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:03.170
It requires judgement.

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:05.390
And see, our standard models are great with

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:07.550
risk, but they just completely fall apart when

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:08.930
they come up against true uncertainty.

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:11.410
So, how in the world do we handle

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:12.370
all this uncertainty?

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:14.910
Well, this is where our hero of the

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:16.890
day comes in, the Monte Carlo simulation.

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:18.570
I want you to think of it like

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:20.970
a powerful engine that is specifically designed to

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:24.290
navigate uncertainty by exploring thousands, sometimes even millions

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:25.310
of possible futures.

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:28.070
Now, the process sounds complex, but the idea

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:29.270
is actually pretty straightforward.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:31.490
First, instead of guessing that our food truck

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:33.630
will sell exactly 100 meals a day, we

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:34.390
define a range.

00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:35.870
We might say, you know, it could be

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:37.210
anywhere from 60 to 140.

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:39.050
We do that for all of our key

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:39.470
variables.

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:41.930
Then, we let the computer take over and

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:43.770
run the simulation thousands of times.

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:46.070
On each run, it just randomly picks a

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:48.050
value from each of those ranges and calculates

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:49.650
the profit for that specific scenario.

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:52.310
And finally, it gathers up all those thousands

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:54.550
of different profit numbers to build one complete

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:54.890
picture.

00:03:55.430 --> 00:03:56.510
Think about that for a second.

00:03:56.890 --> 00:03:59.070
Every single one of those runs, that's one

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:00.450
plausible version of the future.

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:02.570
By running thousands of them, we're not trying

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:04.710
to predict the future with a capital T.

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:06.870
What we're actually doing is mapping out the

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:08.410
entire landscape of what's possible.

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:10.890
And this is where we get the big

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:12.050
aha moment.

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:15.130
We're about to see the massive, dramatic difference

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:18.089
between that single, fragile number we started with

00:04:18.089 --> 00:04:21.130
and the rich, insightful picture that a simulation

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:21.850
gives us.

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:22.930
Remember this?

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:25.950
Our old view, one number, looks so confident,

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:27.010
so precise.

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:30.070
And as we now know, it's totally misleading.

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:32.230
Okay, now let's see what the Monte Carlo

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:33.590
simulation actually revealed.

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:35.790
And here it is, the new view.

00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:37.770
Instead of one single point on a chart,

00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:40.050
we get a full distribution, a complete picture

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:40.730
of the possibilities.

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:43.150
Now we can see that, yeah, a modest

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:45.370
profit is the most likely outcome, but we

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:47.130
can also see there's a 20% chance

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:48.210
of actually losing money.

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:50.370
And there's also a 15% chance of

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:50.950
hitting it big.

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:53.410
This is the critical info we were completely

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:54.190
blind to before.

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:57.230
To put it even more simply, the simulation

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:59.290
is telling us that based on our best

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:01.790
assumptions, there's about an 80% chance of

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:04.130
making a profit and a 20% chance

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:04.950
of posting a loss.

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:08.990
That single number, that 75 grand, it completely

00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:11.610
and totally hid this fundamental risk from us.

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:14.030
And we can get even more specific with

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:16.610
this using a concept called percentiles.

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:18.810
It's really just a simple way to answer

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:22.110
that nagging question, how bad could things realistically

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:22.450
get?

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:25.550
For instance, the fifth percentile outcome is a

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:28.270
number so low that 95% of all

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:29.910
possible futures are better than it.

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:31.450
It's how we can actually put a number

00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:32.690
on our downside risk.

00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:35.070
Okay, so this brings us to our final

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:37.110
and honestly most important section.

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:39.910
How do we actually use this new, richer

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:42.590
understanding of uncertainty to make smarter, more resilient

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:44.770
decisions from data to action?

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:46.510
Let's put this into practise.

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:49.730
Imagine we're also considering a coffee cart project.

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:52.790
Now, a standard analysis shows that both the

00:05:52.790 --> 00:05:54.830
food truck and the coffee cart have the

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:58.410
exact same average expected profit, $75,000.

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:00.530
On paper, they look identical.

00:06:00.950 --> 00:06:03.250
But when we run the simulation, a totally

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:04.430
different story emerges.

00:06:04.910 --> 00:06:07.990
The coffee cart's fifth percentile outcome, it's realistically

00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:10.590
bad scenario, is still a profit, and its

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:12.370
chance of losing any money at all is

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:13.390
only 5%.

00:06:13.390 --> 00:06:15.990
The food truck is obviously a much riskier

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:16.230
bet.

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:18.530
Now, notice something really important here.

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:20.950
The simulation didn't tell us which project to

00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:21.190
pick.

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:22.470
It's not a magic eight ball.

00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:24.270
What it did was give us a clear,

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:26.370
honest picture of the risk profile for each

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:26.770
choice.

00:06:27.170 --> 00:06:28.770
It gives us the clarity we need to

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:29.470
use our judgement.

00:06:30.070 --> 00:06:31.590
Maybe we're willing to take on the higher

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:32.990
risk of the food truck for that higher

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:35.690
potential reward, or maybe we prefer the safer

00:06:35.690 --> 00:06:36.610
bet of the coffee cart.

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:38.750
The point is, now we can make that

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:40.290
choice with our eyes wide open.

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:42.890
And this final quote is just a fantastic,

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:45.010
humble reminder of what this is all about.

00:06:45.390 --> 00:06:48.410
Simulation remains a tool for organising ignorance, not

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:49.190
eliminating it.

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:51.430
We can never eliminate the uncertainty of the

00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:53.710
future, but a simulation allows us to structure

00:06:53.710 --> 00:06:55.850
our thinking about it, to organise our ignorance,

00:06:56.110 --> 00:06:58.190
and to make disciplined choices right in the

00:06:58.190 --> 00:06:58.690
face of it.

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:00.690
So if you take just one thing away

00:07:00.690 --> 00:07:02.390
from all of this, let it be this.

00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:04.310
It's time to shift your thinking.

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:07.350
Stop demanding a single, precise answer that is

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:10.150
almost always wrong anyway, and start seeking to

00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:12.530
understand the full range of what's truly possible.

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:15.210
And that leaves us with one final question

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:16.110
for you to think about.

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:18.890
What single number in your job, in your

00:07:18.890 --> 00:07:21.550
finances, or in your life might be giving

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:23.990
you a false sense of certainty right now?

00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:28.330
What complex, messy, and important reality might it

00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:28.750
be hiding?

